  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Myanmar - ACJR 2024 (Project)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">By the provision of cash, KBC and Plan aim to meet the acute and multisectoral needs identified in the four townships that will be targeted. Recipients will be supported to access necessary food commodities. Protection elements will be mainstreamed throughout this assistance, aiming to ensure a Do No Harm approach and aiming to meet some of the specific needs of children and adolescents, especially those who face protection risks exacerbated by household financial vulnerability.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Communities in Kachin State have been affected by armed conflict since 2011, causing a large number of Internally Displaced Persons (IDP). Many IDP camps are located in Myitkyina, Waingmaw, Mogaung and Mohnyin townships. While there were initial plans for resettlement of IDPs, these plans were put on hold because of COVID and the new Government that came to power afterwards. Armed conflict resumed from late 2021 onwards, increasing the number of IDPs in the respected area. The situation of these IDPs due to the protracted conflict was exacerbated by the floods that recently took place, namely at the end of June 2024. The flooding has caused a surge in the prices of food and other essentials. This has made it challenging for the affected population to access basic supplies, including food resources to prepare meals. The flooding has also affected the livelihoods of many people, especially farmers in the camps and villages, and most cattle, livestock and farming activities are affected. The most vulnerable communities, especially women, child-headed households and casual labourers, are facing difficulties such as lower income, no availability of farming equipment and the death of cattle in the affected areas.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">KBC and Plan will conduct door-to-door verifications and community consultations before distributing cash to 1,768 of the most vulnerable households, providing them with a one-time assistance of 320,000 MMK. Safety measures will be in place and the distribution will be followed by Post-Distribution Monitoring. This multipurpose cash round is complemented by a targeted cash-for-food response, providing two rounds of 43,000 MMK for the most vulnerable household members such as young children, pregnant and lactating women and persons with disabilities. Recipients will be supported to access necessary food commodities and to meet the standard of 2100 kcal per person per day. Awareness raising will be done on childhood nutrition simultaneous to the distribution, and it will again be followed by Post-Distribution Monitoring including interviews with specific recipients. 

The cash element will also include protection mainstreaming activities such as training for staff on safeguarding, PSHEA, child protection and how to identify child protection violations, a safeguarding risk assessment and mitigation planning in consultation with children, women, men and community leaders, awareness raising on safeguarding and PSHEA including expected behaviours from staff and mechanisms to report any incident, Psychological First Aid training for distribution team members, outreach to the most vulnerable groups of cash recipients who have specific challenges and needs in collecting and using cash and protection risks they face, a Protection Help Desk that operates during cash distribution to provide information to recipients (e.g. on nutrition), and monitoring of cash distribution safetiness by protection staff and volunteers using the inter-agency safe distribution checklists adapted to the context and follow up.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Protracted and new IDPs displaced through armed conflict and recent floods, prioritising:
- Children and adolescents
- Women (specifically pregnant and lactating women)
- People with disabilities
- Child/women-headed households</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SUMMARY

While Plan initially aimed to carry out the Acute Joint Response with a Protection and Multipurpose Cash component in another area of Myanmar, increased restrictions hindered the start of implementation. In August, a change of project location was approved, enabling Plan and local partner KBC to start implementation in Kachin State. Through Multipurpose Cash activities, without a standalone Protection component but with Protection mainstreamed throughout its approach, Plan and KBC aim to reach a total of 8,267 IDPs in four townships affected by conflict and recent floods.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">With support from the START fund, Plan and KBC responded to the flood that took place at the end of June, jointly providing cash assistance to reach vulnerable households in the affected areas. This project ended in August, and with limited resources only a selected number of households received cash assistance. Through this Acute Joint Response, Plan and KBC are able to extend this support to more vulnerable and new IDPs. In line with the CHS and the Alliance for CPHA CP-FSL integration guidance, strong protection mainstreaming will also be put in place through protection helpdesks, safeguarding/PSEA trainings and awareness raising on key protection issues.</narrative>
  </description>
